WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.169
-Our next guest is a
very funny comedian and actor

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.270
you know from films like
"Train Wreck"

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.438
and "Don't Think Twice,"

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:07.707
as well as the
Showtime series "Billions."

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:10.010 line:0%
His Broadway show --
it's fantastic --

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.277 line:0%
"The Old man &amp; the Pool,"
is playing at

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:12.812 line:0%
the Vivian Beaumont Theater,

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:15.014 line:0%
with an additional
two-week extension of its run.

00:00:15.014 --> 00:00:18.284
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Mike Birbiglia.

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:20.220
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:37.136
Welcome back.
-So good to see you.

00:00:37.136 --> 00:00:38.938
-It's so good to see you.
I saw you last night.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:43.576
My mom and I came and saw
your show, and it was fantastic.

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.278
-Thank you so much.
It's been such a blast.

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:49.815
And I insisted on taking
a photo with your mom.

00:00:49.815 --> 00:00:50.850
-You did, yeah.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.485
-I was sort of the Patty
of the scenario.

00:00:53.485 --> 00:00:55.087
-Yeah, you --
-And the Bruce.

00:00:55.087 --> 00:00:57.790
I was the Patty and the Bruce.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.460
-Yeah, I just -- Because, again,
we just showed this, right?

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:01.494
-Right.

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:02.995
-You sure you want me
to show it?

00:01:02.995 --> 00:01:05.565
-Yeah, I think it's --
-Alright.

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:10.169
I mean, she's pretty happy.
No, no, she's happy.

00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:11.838
She's happy. That's her --

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:13.206
-We got it at a wrong moment.

00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:16.643
-Yeah, but it's -- Look at the
side-by-side. I mean...

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:18.378
-It's like you
can't tell her apart.

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:20.913
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.913 --> 00:01:23.249
You also --
So, you've been doing

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.084
shows like this for a long time.

00:01:25.084 --> 00:01:26.886
And I have been
coming to them for a long time

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:28.854
and enjoying them a great deal.

00:01:28.854 --> 00:01:31.324
And I did not realize,
you found photos of me

00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:32.825
from 10 years ago,
at the premiere --

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:33.993
-I did this thing.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:35.295
So, my first show was
"Sleepwalk with Me."

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:37.363
My second one was
"My Girlfriend's Boyfriend."

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:38.431
And I dug this one up from the

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:40.333
"My Girlfriend's Boyfriend"
premiere.

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:42.568
I just -- I put in Seth's name

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:45.871
into my photos program,
and what showed up was this.

00:01:45.871 --> 00:01:47.640
-This is me, I guess, waiting in
line for the bathroom.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.208
-Yep. Waiting in line
for the bathroom.

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:50.910
These are real. 2011.
-These are real photos.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.947
-I like -- Why did you take
a picture of me waiting in line?

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:56.849
-No, these are -- I just have a
million photos from there.

00:01:56.849 --> 00:01:59.885
-Alright. That's me and my wife,
Alexi, at your show.

00:01:59.885 --> 00:02:01.354
And this is --

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:05.258
-By the way, pretending to pay
attention to the conversation.

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:07.627
-Yeah. I'm the dead giveaway.

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:10.062
You can always tell when Seth
is kind of not into it.

00:02:10.062 --> 00:02:12.765
-Hold on.
Can I just do an impression?

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:17.202
You want to leave
the conversation.

00:02:17.202 --> 00:02:20.006
-This is really -- You know,
this is a triumphant --

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:21.307
This was, you know,
opening night, and --

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:23.809
-Yes, opening night of
"My Girlfriend's Boyfriend."

00:02:23.809 --> 00:02:25.010
-And this is just
me and John Hodgman,

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:26.345
We're so proud of you.

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:28.982
And you just look great
in this picture right there.

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:30.850
-This is...

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.851
And for some reason,
the photo is taken

00:02:32.851 --> 00:02:36.956
behind the photographers.

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:40.759
And you're just, like, teasing
me and holding up my hands.

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:44.563
This would never be used in any
kind of magazine or anything.

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:46.599
Who would ever use this photo?

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:48.134
-This is -- The fact
that you used it here

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:49.501
is an incredible breakthrough.

00:02:49.501 --> 00:02:53.039
This is -- Oh, also you --

00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:54.340
You know, I obviously came to
see your show.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.474
There were
other people backstage

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:56.976
who came to see your show.

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:58.344
Paul Rudd came to see your show.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:01.614
And you have -- Some people
have sort of mentioned

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:03.382
that you might look
a little like Paul Rudd.

00:03:03.382 --> 00:03:05.484
-Over the years, people go,
"You look like Paul Rudd,"

00:03:05.484 --> 00:03:07.853
and then, sometimes,
they dial it back and they go,

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:09.088
"You look like Paul Rudd.

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:11.056
He's a little better-looking."

00:03:11.056 --> 00:03:12.225
You know what I mean?

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:14.226
And then they go like,
"He's in better --"

00:03:14.226 --> 00:03:16.462
"Ant-Man" hasn't helped.
-No.

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:18.665
-You know what I mean?
He's buff, you know?

00:03:18.665 --> 00:03:20.165
-Yeah. And it
could have gone either way.

00:03:20.165 --> 00:03:21.334
You could have been Ant-Man.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:22.735
It could have been me,
could have been him.

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:27.072
And then, a few years ago,
I did my last show,

00:03:27.072 --> 00:03:28.607
which was called "The New One,"

00:03:28.607 --> 00:03:30.142
at the Ahmanson Theatre
in Los Angeles,

00:03:30.142 --> 00:03:32.779
and a reviewer came
and the reviewer wrote,

00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:34.079
in the "Los Angeles Times,"

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:39.986
"If this becomes a film,
it must star Paul Rudd."

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:43.356
I'm an actor also!

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:47.560
I make films also!

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:52.031
A cursory look at my Wikipedia

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:55.267
would tell you enough to know,
you know,

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.703
maybe don't mention
another person

00:03:57.703 --> 00:04:02.341
who is roughly the same age
and does the same thing

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:03.475
for the same --
Anyway, you get the idea.

00:04:03.475 --> 00:04:05.878
-Now, is this staged,
this photo,

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:08.380
or is this just proof of how
much you look like Paul Rudd?

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.783
'Cause look --
-Isn't that nice?

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:11.884
-That's really a great photo.

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:13.418
-Our photographer, Emilio,
did that.

00:04:13.418 --> 00:04:14.954
And it's a trick shot.

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:17.323
Because I said to Paul --
He came to the show.

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:19.024
And I go, "I get this comparison
all the time,

00:04:19.024 --> 00:04:22.161
and I think it would be
funny for my Instagram

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:25.898
if I said -- like, when
I'm feeling down about myself,

00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:29.001
I imagine I'm Paul Rudd."

00:04:29.001 --> 00:04:30.069
And then, he was like, "Yeah."

00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:31.738
And so we each
stood in the same spot

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:32.872
and then we Photoshopped --

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:34.140
-It's very well done.
-It's fun, right?

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.208
-It's very well done.
-Yeah.

00:04:35.208 --> 00:04:37.710
-So, your show is so funny,

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.245
which is what
I've come to expect from you.

00:04:39.245 --> 00:04:41.581
What's a little different is,
this show is about

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:44.650
some serious
bad-health diagnoses

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.085
you've got from a doctor.

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:47.553
So, when you're
going through that --

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:50.189
Because from the very first time
you see a doctor

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:51.423
in the telling of the show,

00:04:51.423 --> 00:04:52.791
you have very funny
observations.

00:04:52.791 --> 00:04:54.060
Is that happening in real time?

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.662
When you're getting bad news,
are you also like,

00:04:55.662 --> 00:04:57.029
"Oh, but also,
that's going to be fun

00:04:57.029 --> 00:04:58.263
to talk about
and this over here"?

00:04:58.263 --> 00:05:00.933
-Everything in my
life all the time is like,

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:02.067
"That could be in."

00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:03.336
You know what I mean?

00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:06.739
Like, I fell down subway steps
a few years ago --

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:08.441
And, literally,
I fell down the steps.

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:10.910
I broke my shoulder.
I'm lying on the floor.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:14.079
People are blowing past me like,
"You need help? No? Good."

00:05:14.079 --> 00:05:15.214
You know what I mean?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:16.682
And you're laughing
because you've been

00:05:16.682 --> 00:05:19.952
one of these pigs,
and I want you pigs to know,

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:23.923
we of the floor are aware
you do not wish to help.

00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:26.359
But, literally, I'm lying on
the floor thinking,

00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:27.660
"This could be
pretty good material."

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.429
You know what I mean?
I'm always thinking that.

00:05:30.429 --> 00:05:32.598
And then, with this show,
I talk about it.

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:34.266
Like, "Hi.
"I have type 2 diabetes.

00:05:34.266 --> 00:05:35.435
I had bladder cancer.

00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:37.502
I have a serious
sleepwalking disorder."

00:05:37.502 --> 00:05:40.872
Sometimes, I'm onstage
and I am thinking like, "Wow!

00:05:40.872 --> 00:05:43.376
That's a lot of stuff.

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:44.910
This guy has got it rough."

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:48.948
And then I'm like,
"This guy's me."

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:51.283
So, yeah, no, it's a weird --
It's kind of the inverse

00:05:51.283 --> 00:05:52.918
of that, where sometimes,
I'm like,

00:05:52.918 --> 00:05:55.821
"Oh, maybe have
I revealed too much?"

00:05:55.821 --> 00:05:58.457
But then, ultimately, like,
people come to the show.

00:05:58.457 --> 00:06:00.993
I talk about, like, the darkest
things about my own experience,

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:02.295
the saddest things.

00:06:02.295 --> 00:06:04.330
I make jokes of them.
I talk about death.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:05.364
It's a lot about death.

00:06:05.364 --> 00:06:06.666
And people
feel better afterwards,

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:08.067
and, like, it's honestly --

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:10.870
Like, performing all these
years -- it's my fifth show --

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.639
it's the one
that makes me feel like

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:17.076
I'm doing a thing
that's helping a little bit.

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:18.511
-Yeah. I felt that way.

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:22.047
I mean, it is a --
Again, I want to stress,

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:24.183
it's very funny
from start to finish,

00:06:24.183 --> 00:06:25.884
but there are moments
where you sort of --

00:06:25.884 --> 00:06:28.020
You talk to us honestly
about when

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.555
you were taking stock
of your life.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:30.822
And I think it's
a really helpful thing

00:06:30.822 --> 00:06:32.591
to be there listening to.

00:06:32.591 --> 00:06:34.560
I have more to ask you about,

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.197
especially how the YMCA has
responded to the show so far.

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:41.334
-Oh, yes, the YMCA
has not made a formal response,

00:06:41.334 --> 00:06:42.635
but, yeah,
we'll talk about that.

00:06:42.635 --> 00:06:44.904
-We'll be right back
with more from Mike Birbiglia.

